1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:07,849
Hello, I am Gagan N, USN 24BTRBT013,
and this is the live demonstration of my

2
00:00:07,849 --> 00:00:14,336
project, Secure Peer-to-Peer Communication
between Enterprise Corda Nodes.

3
00:00:14,286 --> 00:00:18,452
Everything you see here is
running live on my laptop.

4
00:00:18,452 --> 00:00:24,018
First, here is the project structure:
the contracts module with my state and

5
00:00:24,018 --> 00:00:29,657
contract, the workflows module with my
flows, the crypto helper and the trust

6
00:00:29,657 --> 00:00:32,000
policy, and the RPC demo client.

7
00:00:33,713 --> 00:00:36,175
Next, I run the tests.

8
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:40,029
Gradle runs the fifteen
contract tests live now.

9
00:00:40,029 --> 00:00:45,869
The seven MockNetwork flow tests were run
in full just before this recording, so

10
00:00:45,869 --> 00:00:48,351
Gradle reports them as up to date.

11
00:00:48,351 --> 00:00:54,271
The summary shows all twenty-two tests
passing, including the privacy, integrity,

12
00:00:54,271 --> 00:00:56,755
trust policy and ciphertext tests.

13
00:01:23,253 --> 00:01:29,490
Now I start the four Corda nodes: the
notary, EnterpriseA, EnterpriseB and

14
00:01:29,490 --> 00:01:30,501
EnterpriseC.

15
00:01:30,451 --> 00:01:37,213
Each node runs headless as its own Java
process, with its own P2P and RPC ports, and

16
00:01:37,213 --> 00:01:41,963
my script waits for the started
up line in each node's log.

17
00:01:41,963 --> 00:01:47,777
Starting a Corda node takes around a
minute, because it loads the CorDapps, opens

18
00:01:47,777 --> 00:01:53,807
its vault database, starts the Artemis
message broker and registers with the network

19
00:01:53,807 --> 00:01:54,094
map.

20
00:01:54,094 --> 00:02:00,550
While they start, remember my trust policy:
EnterpriseA trusts only EnterpriseB, so

21
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:03,272
EnterpriseC will be rejected later.

22
00:02:52,937 --> 00:02:55,339
All four nodes are up.

23
00:02:55,289 --> 00:03:01,955
Here is the network map as EnterpriseA
sees it: three enterprise nodes and the

24
00:03:01,955 --> 00:03:07,766
non-validating notary, each with
its X.500 name and its P2P address.

25
00:03:10,559 --> 00:03:12,592
Now the certificates.

26
00:03:12,542 --> 00:03:16,697
I run keytool on
EnterpriseA's three keystores.

27
00:03:16,697 --> 00:03:22,011
The TLS key store holds a chain of
four certificates, from the node's TLS

28
00:03:22,011 --> 00:03:27,471
certificate through the node CA and
the Doorman CA up to the Corda root CA.

29
00:03:27,471 --> 00:03:34,637
The identity key is Ed25519, and the
trust store contains only the root CA.

30
00:03:36,387 --> 00:03:44,229
Next, I test the P2P port with openssl,
using TLS 1.2 and no client certificate.

31
00:03:44,179 --> 00:03:50,144
The server presents EnterpriseA's certificate
and asks for a client certificate.

32
00:03:50,144 --> 00:03:54,644
Because I have none, the
handshake fails with alert forty.

33
00:03:54,644 --> 00:04:01,727
With a real network certificate the
handshake completes, and TLS 1.3 is refused.

34
00:04:01,727 --> 00:04:04,775
So mutual TLS is enforced.

35
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:08,388
Now the main part.

36
00:04:08,338 --> 00:04:14,459
EnterpriseA sends three confidential
messages to EnterpriseB, and B sends one

37
00:04:14,459 --> 00:04:15,493
invoice back.

38
00:04:15,493 --> 00:04:23,379
For each message, the flow runs an X25519
key exchange over the session, encrypts

39
00:04:23,379 --> 00:04:31,362
the body with AES-256-GCM, collects both
signatures and notarises the transaction.

40
00:04:31,362 --> 00:04:36,005
The table shows that only
ciphertext is stored on the ledger.

41
00:04:38,855 --> 00:04:42,864
Next, each receiver
acknowledges its messages.

42
00:04:42,814 --> 00:04:49,077
Before acknowledging, the receiver decrypts
the message and verifies its SHA-256

43
00:04:49,077 --> 00:04:49,469
hash.

44
00:04:49,469 --> 00:04:52,921
Then EnterpriseB reads the purchase order.

45
00:04:52,921 --> 00:04:58,066
The decrypted body appears, and the hash
on the ledger matches the hash of the

46
00:04:58,066 --> 00:05:01,100
decrypted text, so the
integrity check passes.

47
00:05:02,793 --> 00:05:05,184
This is the privacy proof.

48
00:05:05,134 --> 00:05:11,233
EnterpriseA and EnterpriseB each have eight
message states: the consumed SENT states

49
00:05:11,233 --> 00:05:13,193
plus the ACKNOWLEDGED ones.

50
00:05:13,193 --> 00:05:15,681
EnterpriseC has zero.

51
00:05:15,681 --> 00:05:21,598
It was never a participant, so it never
received any of these transactions.

52
00:05:23,271 --> 00:05:27,745
Now EnterpriseC tries to send
a message to EnterpriseA.

53
00:05:27,695 --> 00:05:32,923
EnterpriseA's responder flow checks
its trusted peers list, does not find

54
00:05:32,923 --> 00:05:36,790
EnterpriseC, and throws the
exception: Untrusted peer.

55
00:05:36,790 --> 00:05:39,099
No state is created.

56
00:05:45,685 --> 00:05:48,992
Next, I measure the P2P latency.

57
00:05:48,942 --> 00:05:54,304
PingPeerFlow does twenty send and
receive round trips from EnterpriseA to

58
00:05:54,304 --> 00:06:00,474
EnterpriseB over the TLS link, and the table
shows the average, minimum, maximum and

59
00:06:00,474 --> 00:06:02,751
median latency in milliseconds.

60
00:06:07,504 --> 00:06:10,740
To finish, I stop all four nodes.

61
00:06:10,690 --> 00:06:16,666
That completes my live demonstration of
secure, private and verifiable peer-to-peer

62
00:06:16,666 --> 00:06:19,618
messaging between enterprise Corda nodes.

63
00:06:19,618 --> 00:06:21,666
Thank you for watching.

